{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"e01687d8-39e4-4f92-932f-19adbdb5612b","step_index":14,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.076153,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"find notes/ -type f -exec head -n 30 {} +"},"output":"==\u003e notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md \u003c==\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\r\n\r\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\r\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\r\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\r\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\r\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\r\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\r\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\r\n\r\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\r\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\r\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\r\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\r\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\r\neveryone.\r\n\r\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\r\n\r\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\r\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\r\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `\u003cpath\u003e` instead — guaranteed\r\n  everywhere.\r\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\r\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\r\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\r\n\r\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\r\n\r\n==\u003e notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md \u003c==\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\r\n\r\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\r\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\r\nit ran at all.\r\n\r\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\r\n\r\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/\u003cjob-label\u003e`. It showed\r\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\r\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\r\ndebugging \"launchd is not firing\" when the truth was \"my script fails and cannot\r\ntell me.\"\r\n\r\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\r\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\"Input must be\r\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\r\nstdin — `print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\r\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\r\n\r\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\r\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\r\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\r\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\r\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\r\ndeep.\r\n\r\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\r\n\r\n==\u003e notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md \u003c==\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\r\n\r\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\r\ndatabase, no build step.\r\n\r\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\r\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\r\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\r\n\r\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\r\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\r\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\r\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\r\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\r\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\r\nconfusion.\r\n\r\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\r\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\r\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\r\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\r\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\r\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\r\n\r\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\r\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\r\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\r\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\r\n"}}}
